A||||7||Gokart||Wheres it coming from?||||5||
Z||000000||Gokart||12-11-1999||08:56 PM||||I thought I just took care of my small oil leak when I changed my oil pan gasket, no question about it, I did have a small leak coming from there, but I'm still leaky! I degreased the engine and all areas then the next day I could see a small trail coming from the bottom right valve cover by the last bolt, but the biggest spot is a little puddle that sits to the FAR LEFT in between the plenum and the lower intake manifold and drips all the way down to the starter! Any ideas? TIA!||||reg||5
Z||000001||2birds||12-11-1999||09:39 PM||||Distributor tube to block gasket, actually it's an o-ring that hardens over time.  Paid almost $3 at the dealer for mine last Thursday, since nobody else carries one in this area.  Last one cost me 90 cents from Napa, though.  Easy fix?||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||12-11-1999||10:58 PM||||$%^&<BR>I have the same problem. I replaced the distributor o-ring and it didn't help much.  Turns out it is the firewall side valve cover on mine.  I should have replaced the gasket on it while the engine was out but, I'm a moron and forgot about it until now.(banging head on desk)||||reg||8
Z||000003||2birds||12-12-1999||12:39 AM||||Don't feel bad, I pulled the whole intake manifold system thinking it was the lower manifold end seals leaking, when it turned out to be the distributor o-ring.  Live and learn.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Gokart||12-12-1999||06:22 AM||||Is the O-ring job an easy fix? Maybe I can get to that when I also change the vavle cover gasket!||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||12-12-1999||02:51 PM||||When I changed mine is pretty straight forward.  Takes maybe an hour for the hole thing. Here's the steps as I best remember:<BR>1.)disconnect battery<BR>2.)remove dist. cap, and wires going to dist.<BR>3.)note which way rotor is pionting(give yourself a reference mark)<BR>4.)remove the bolt and little metal arm that hold the dist. in place<BR>5.)pull straight up on distributor<BR>6.)replace o-ring<BR>7.)slide dist. back down lining the rotor up with original position<BR>8.)put on dist. cap and wires<BR>9.)try to line up distributor shaft as abest as possible to original postition.<BR>10.)adjust timing<BR>11.) lock dist. down with the bolt and arm<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-12-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Gokart||12-12-1999||06:14 PM||||Do I really need to have it re-timed if I don't turn the crankshaft?||||reg||1
Z||000007||2birds||12-12-1999||09:56 PM||||Yes, unless you can check your matchmark with a magnifying glass.  It's easy to do if you have access to a timing light.  One hint, before you start, put some white paint on the balancer to hilite the timing marks.  It makes it easier for the strobe light to show the degree marks.||||reg||6
